  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "bEXEMBu1UfdGnKQHOryEwjR1+G4=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:24:42.260Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Caluma is a form-and-workflow toolkit: it defines what a case looks like and the states it moves through. The gap the research identifies is narrow and specific \u2014 the workflow state itself is mutable. The current status is stored and updated in place, so what the workflow decided at each step is not preserved as a separate record that cannot be rewritten.\n\nThe commercial shape here is unusual within the corpus. Adfinis wrote Caluma, so this is not a pitch aimed at a tool the firm happens to use; it is co-development on their own product. The gap is theirs to close, closing it improves something they sell, and that changes both who has to be convinced and what the deliverable looks like.\n\nIt also raises the bar on precision. When the counterpart authored the tool, a description of its limits has to be exactly right or the credibility of the entire argument goes with it. The claim to make is the one the research supports \u2014 the workflow state is mutable \u2014 without inflating it into a verdict on the rest of Caluma.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**1 \u00b7 start here**\n- Adfinis AG (CH Bern/Basel/Zurich + DE/NL) \u2014 Michael Moser, Chairman of the Board \u00b7 info@adfinis.com \u00b7 https://adfinis.com\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Caluma builds the form and the workflow; the workflow state itself is mutable. Adfinis WROTE Caluma, so this is co-development on their own product, not a pitch at it.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:24:42.260434Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:24:42.260439Z"
    }
  },
